WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:03.350 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  a pittsburgh synagogue devastated by a  mass shooting  is looking to rebuild itself for a  brighter future  you may remember that a hate-filled  gunman stormed into the tree of life  synagogue  during saturday morning services almost  three years ago  11 people were killed and six others  hurt  now one of the world's best known  architects aims to transform the site of  tragedy  into a place that inspires hope our jim  axelrod spoke with the architect  and the congregation's rabbi jim good  morning good morning and it was a deep  and meaningful conversation anthony the  architect the rabbi  and every member of the tree of life  congregation are being guided by three  words as they try to plan a way forward  for the scene of this unspeakable horror  remember rebuild and renew  in pittsburgh the architect daniel  liebuskind is beginning his latest  project like so much of his other work  this one too  is rooted in devastation he walked  through a site  of murder of a mass murder of the  greatest attack on  on jews in this country liebeskind who  navigated complex emotional terrain  designing the ground zero site  and the jewish museum in berlin will now  lead the rebuild of the tree of life  campus  closed since the shooting on october 27  2018  when a hate-filled gunman murdered 11  and  injured six during shabbat services it's  not just a walk-through  disaster you walk through the memory  of what happened and you think of the  significance what will this mean  what does it tell us liebeskind the son  of holocaust survivors recently made his  first trip to pittsburgh to meet with  rabbi jeffrey meyers and members of the  congregation  the drawings are done here my father did  the job  examine artifacts of the day and begin  to consider  a way forward how do you decide whether  you knock it all down because  it's just too full of horror you have to  walk through  and imagine we're traces of importance  on the side  what is not just salvageable but  inspires us  still and how do we document the past  as it falls unfolds to a brighter future  it's a delicate balancing act  marking trauma while wrestling hope  from the pain one of the if not the  governing principles  will be making sure everyone is reminded  that there is more light than darkness  that's absolutely true  light has to be than the message of the  place because  we do live in darkness a lot of the time  and we saw the darkness and the evil  of the events that befell on this  congregation  but i think a building has to do  something else has to  has to give you a sense of inspiration a  breath of life  and there's an excitement of well  we can't wait to see what this will  become  rabbi myers took us for a walk past the  site they have optimism for a bright  future  pointing to artwork sent from around the  world including students at marjory  stoneman douglas high school that hope  can come out of the horror of  of what happened at that school to me  that's just incredible  it's provided inspiration about a  direction for the project  i've come to learn that through two and  a half years  through letters emails cards phone calls  from people around the world complete  strangers there's this vast silent  majority out there in the world  who are good decent people yeah the time  has come  that the silent majority become a vocal  majority to say to the rest of the world  this is unacceptable you cannot deny  what happened on that site  you have to be able to integrate in a  meaningful way while it's early in the  process  daniel liebuskin rabbi myers and the  tree of life community  are beginning to frame a mission  building a beachhead in the battle  between tolerance and hate what stories  will you be telling with the design and  construction  of the site at the tree of life  the tree of life will not be meister it  will be story of the people  who are there on that day it'll be the  story of  beauty of fatality of death but also of  rebirth  of renewal of inspiration  as daniel liebeska knows all too well  from the complex  challenges his other projects have  presented to him it takes time  to figure out the right balance between  remembering rebuilding and renewing  we're told the design process is  expected to be completed and ground  should be broken  by early next year yeah i love the  message light has to be the message of  the place  so that was to me the most moving part  of the whole interview when rabbi myers  you could hear his voice  in the last bite of the uh the piece  there that  that that there is a silent majority of  good decent people that need to become a  vocal majority  this campus the tree of life campus  needs to be the place  where that idea can take longer what  they say at the tree of life itself  already is  more light equals more life yeah well if  daniel liebskin can can  design a jewish museum in berlin you  know he can his work is always about  resilience yeah  ground zero as well i just think about  the strength he must have to constantly  be working in the space of all this  tragedy and turning it into something  hopeful  absolutely thank you very much jim

